WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.045 --> 00:00:01.670
NICHOLE CLARKE: Hi,
I'm Nichole Clarke,

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:04.542
and my project is working
on developing the iOS

00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:06.500
version of App Inventor
in order to democratize

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.322
cross-platform
mobile development.

00:00:08.322 --> 00:00:10.280
Now, a big thing about
the App Inventor is that

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.020
it's block-based
visual programming,

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.640
and so that means that when
you're developing [? an ?]

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.056
[? Android ?] application, you
don't actually need to know

00:00:16.056 --> 00:00:17.457
Java or anything of the like.

00:00:17.457 --> 00:00:19.040
Now, when it comes
to iOS development,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.290
there's still very
large barriers to entry.

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:22.940
One, you need to know Swift.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.280
Two, you need to have
an iOS or Apple device,

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.720
such as a Mac laptop.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.670
And three, the cost of owning
that device is pretty high.

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.970
So what we wanted to do
is develop and democratize

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.210
not only Android development,
but also iOS development.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.110
And that's what my
project is working on.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.710
I'm working on developing the
components for the iOS version

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.420
in the native language of Swift.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.910
That way, what you're able to
do is take your existing App

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.800
Inventor project
and then compile it

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.540
into either an iOS
or Android device.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.540
Now, what I worked on was
developing components.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:52.540
And inherently there were
a couple of challenges

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.500
because the Android
version already existed.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.840
For one, you want to really
keep the Android version

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:57.754
as the standard.

00:00:57.754 --> 00:01:00.170
So it meant that when were
doing things like the map view,

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.750
we had to ensure that each
tile overlay match already

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.069
existed on Android.

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:06.881
And for iOS, that meant adding
in a custom tile overlay.

00:01:06.881 --> 00:01:09.380
Additionally, we wanted things
like the accelerometer sensor

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.088
to use the same
coordinates, because it'd

00:01:11.088 --> 00:01:13.290
be odd if you ran the same
app on iOS and Android

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.290
but one had up going this
way, and the other one

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.980
had positive going this way.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.042
So you really need to
map that to be the same.

00:01:19.042 --> 00:01:21.500
We also wanted to keep the same
human interfaces that Apple

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.565
had laid out already
and really not

00:01:23.565 --> 00:01:25.940
have it feel as though you're
using something that wasn't

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.734
actually an iOS application.

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:30.150
Then lastly we had to deal
with permission handling, which

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:31.733
is dealing with
permissions at runtime

00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:34.479
and accepting or rejecting
them to follow what Apple did.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:36.770
Ultimately, we've had some
pretty good results, though.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.500
By randomly selecting
1.8 million applications,

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.310
we're currently able to build
65.44% of the applications.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:44.810
And let me put this
in perspective--

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.220
the Android version
of App Inventor

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.053
has been around for
eight years, and we just

00:01:48.053 --> 00:01:50.880
began work on the iOS
version in this past year.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.350
So I'd say we're
doing pretty well.

